<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="The 2013 Scapin K6: an Italian carbon racing bicycle with Campagnolo components and Fulcrum Racing Speed wheels."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/bikes/scapin-k6/hero.webp"/><title>Scapin K6 2013 · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="bikes" danishPath="/cykler/scapin-k6/"/><main class="atala-page"><header class="quick-hero"><p class="section-label">In the collection · Italy · 2013</p><h1>Scapin<br/><em>K6</em></h1><p>A carbon racer from the period when low weight, stiffness, aerodynamics and rider position were developed as one system.</p></header><figure class="quick-pass"><img src={`${images}/hero.webp`} alt="Red, black and white Scapin K6 from 2013"/><figcaption>The carbon frame, Campagnolo groupset and deep Fulcrum wheels typify an early-2010s high-end Italian racer. Photo: The Evolution of Bikes.</figcaption></figure><section class="atala-facts"><div><strong>2013</strong><span>Stated year</span></div><div><strong>Carbon</strong><span>Shaped frame and fork</span></div><div><strong>Campagnolo</strong><span>Mechanical groupset</span></div><div><strong>Fulcrum</strong><span>Racing Speed wheels</span></div></section><section class="atala-story"><div><p class="section-label">Scapin since 1957</p><h2>The craft changed material</h2></div><div><p>Umberto Scapin founded the marque near Padua in 1957. It became known for small-series Italian steel frames in which fit and execution mattered as much as weight.</p><p>Carbon changed both tools and form. Wide, flat or asymmetric shapes could follow loads in ways steel tubes could not. The K6 combines that modern language with the identity of a smaller Italian maker.</p></div></section><section class="atala-details"><figure><img src={`${images}/frame-detail.webp`} alt="Wide carbon down tube"/><figcaption>Carbon permits broad, load-specific surfaces.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/fork-detail.webp`} alt="Fork and head tube"/><figcaption>A compact front with a conventional rim brake.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/cockpit.webp`} alt="FSA cockpit and Campagnolo controls"/><figcaption>Visible cables place it before fully integrated cockpits.</figcaption></figure></section><section class="atala-components"><div><p class="section-label">The technology</p><h2>Low weight without hiding the mechanics</h2></div><div><p>Mechanical Campagnolo controls and rim brakes make the bicycle a snapshot from just before electronic gears and disc brakes changed the road-racing silhouette. Deep carbon Fulcrum wheels shape the side profile as strongly as the frame.</p><p>Frame stiffness, wheel depth, saddle placement and the low front work together to position the rider efficiently.</p></div></section><section class="koga-specs"><div><h2>Brief specification</h2></div><dl><div><dt>Frame and fork</dt><dd>Carbon</dd></div><div><dt>Groupset</dt><dd>Mechanical Campagnolo</dd></div><div><dt>Wheels</dt><dd>Fulcrum Racing Speed</dd></div><div><dt>Status</dt><dd>In the collection</dd></div></dl></section><section class="atala-closing"><p>The K6 shows mature carbon design before the bicycle closed completely around its own technology.</p><div class="bike-bottom-nav"><a href={`${base}/en/bikes/`}>← Back to bicycles</a><a href={`${base}/en/periods/2010-2019/`}>View the period →</a></div></section></main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
